  NEW DEFECT FOUND: a constant, non-source 98.168 Hz tone at -28.7 dBFS spans the entire output, plus its 196 Hz overtone (+5.8 dB over ref). Narrowband tracking at 98 Hz: output per-second levels -28.7 -28.7 -28.7 -28.7 -28.7 -28.9 -28.2 -13.6 -29.7 -15.9 -15.9 -29.3 -33.8 -28.4 dBFS (std 5.94 dB) vs reference -115.4 -91.0 -97.4 -90.2 -79.4 -59.0 -46.4 -11.9 -26.2 -17.8 -17.8 -27.1 -30.7 -47.6 dBFS (std 33.30 dB); mean excess +27.90 dB. It behaves as a hard floor: it only disappears where real content exceeds it. Consequences: quietest 100ms RMS -31.23 dBFS vs reference -62.63 dBFS; dynamic range collapses 57.1 dB -> 25.4 dB; lead-in 0-500 Hz +8.94 dB (500-2000 Hz -0.06 dB, 2000-5000 Hz -0.04 dB => narrowband, not broadband).
  ROOT CAUSE (attributed): output n=2670216, sr=176400, bin spacing 0.066062 Hz. Output's dominant LF partial = 49.0841 Hz = bin 743; injected tone = bin 1486 = 98.168 Hz = exactly 2x the dominant retained bin, i.e. iterative_soft_thresholding's harmonic term working exactly as cycle 3 coded it. The design flaw is that upscale() passes each channel as ONE ~15 s buffer, so the whole-file FFT has a single dominant bin => harmonic_bin is one constant for all 300 iterations => cp.sin(2*pi*harmonic_bin*phase) is a single pure sinusoid of constant amplitude (total 0.1*peak = -20 dBFS) across the entire track, rather than content-tracking detail. Cycle 3 correctly moved it out of the subsonic band, but it is still a single fixed non-source tone -- now audible instead of inaudible.

